Subject: summary.random with Cmatrix
Message-ID: <bp8O2CwM3hM.Hy2L89OQCQAJ@r-inla-discussion-group.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/r-inla-discussion-group/c/bp8O2CwM3hM/m/Hy2L89OQCQAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

How does the interpretation of summary.random change when including a Cmatrix?

For instance, imagine a random intercept model with 10 groups

m1 = inla(y ~  f(group,  model = "iid", constr = T),
              data = dat, family = "gaussian")

After this model has run and we type 'm1$summary.random$group', INLA a intercept for each of the 10 groups

However, when I specify that some groups will be more similar to one another according to a given relationship matrix (sparse_mat), using the "generic2" model and Cmatrix function e.g.

m2 = inla(y ~  f(group,  model = "generic2", constr = T, Cmatrix = sparse_mat),
              data = dat, family = "gaussian")

Running  'm2$summary.random$group', provides us with 20 estimates, 2 per group

So my question, how do we interpret summary.random after including Cmatrix?

Tom

