From: ud7ee34c7589817da at groups.google.com (David Dayi Li)
Date: Tue, 25 Jan 2022 23:14:01 +0000
Subject: How to include smoothing spline for covariate modelling in LGCP
Message-ID: <MRYuQqszlto.9gIR4tG7AQAJ@r-inla-discussion-group.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/r-inla-discussion-group/c/MRYuQqszlto/m/9gIR4tG7AQAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Hi,

I have been reading about the following paper:  Accounting for preferential sampling in species distribution models by Pennino et al.

In their model construction, they mentioned using a Bayesian smoothing spline to model the nonlinear effect of the covariate. I am trying to do a similar thing for my problem but I am not exactly sure how to incorporate a smoothing spline for the covariates in my model. Mostly, I am not familiar with the syntax and there does not seem to be any example code online for doing this.

Can someone provide any help for this? Thank you very much!

Best,
David

From uafb003e064114795 at groups.google.com  Tue Jan 25 23:26:25 2022
From: uafb003e064114795 at groups.google.com (Finn Lindgren)
Date: Tue, 25 Jan 2022 23:26:25 +0000
Subject: Re: [r-inla] How to include smoothing spline for covariate modelling in LGCP
Message-ID: <MRYuQqszlto.gs5gJn-8AQAJ@r-inla-discussion-group.groups.google.com>
In-Reply-To: <MRYuQqszlto.9gIR4tG7AQAJ@r-inla-discussion-group.groups.google.com>
References: <MRYuQqszlto.9gIR4tG7AQAJ@r-inla-discussion-group.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/r-inla-discussion-group/c/MRYuQqszlto/m/gs5gJn-8AQAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Use a rw2 model, which is the continuous Bayesian process model linked to the spline penalty given by the integral of the squared second order derivative. Most inla examples with nonlinear covariate effects use that model, so there should be plenty of examples.

Finn

