Subject: Re: [r-inla] How to include smoothing spline for covariate modelling in LGCP
Message-ID: <MRYuQqszlto.iDhzyKtCAgAJ@r-inla-discussion-group.groups.google.com>
In-Reply-To: <MRYuQqszlto.OYa6ku83AgAJ@r-inla-discussion-group.groups.google.com>
References: <MRYuQqszlto.9gIR4tG7AQAJ@r-inla-discussion-group.groups.google.com> <MRYuQqszlto.OYa6ku83AgAJ@r-inla-discussion-group.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/r-inla-discussion-group/c/MRYuQqszlto/m/iDhzyKtCAgAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Step 1 should be to make sure that you're setting a sensible prior. with scale.model=TRUE and a PC-prior for the standard deviation, you control the prior for the (geometric) average std.dev <http://std.dev>.

Limiting the number of knots to intentionally get an oversmoothed estimate  is a bit of a hack, but you can do something similar by using a 1D spde with 2nd order basis functions; inla.mesh.1d(knots, degree = 2, boundary = "free") with inla.sdpe2.pcmatern() has both a range a nd std.dev <http://std.dev> parameter, but if needed you can fix the range (with prior.range=c(fixedrange, NA))

I have the impression that models where rw2 models overfit are rather due to other problems with those problems, or misinterpretation of the results.
In cases where rw2 models really aren't smooth enough, the more appropriate solution should be to use a rw3 or rw4 model instead. (can be done via generic, but I haven't tried it)

Finn

