Hi Finn,

Thanks for the great advice, I think an rw2 model with good priors sounds like what I need. Picking the sensible priors seems to be the hard part, would you have any links to resources about selecting priors for rw2 when used in this smoothing circumstance?

many thanks,
Sylvan

On Thursday, January 27, 2022 at 4:25:11 PM UTC finn.l...@gmail.com wrote:
> Step 1 should be to make sure that you're setting a sensible prior. with scale.model=TRUE and a PC-prior for the standard deviation, you control the prior for the (geometric) average std.dev <http://std.dev>.
>
> Limiting the number of knots to intentionally get an oversmoothed estimate  is a bit of a hack, but you can do something similar by using a 1D spde with 2nd order basis functions; inla.mesh.1d(knots, degree = 2, boundary = "free") with inla.sdpe2.pcmatern() has both a range a nd std.dev <http://std.dev> parameter, but if needed you can fix the range (with prior.range=c(fixedrange, NA))
>
> I have the impression that models where rw2 models overfit are rather due to other problems with those problems, or misinterpretation of the results.
> In cases where rw2 models really aren't smooth enough, the more appropriate solution should be to use a rw3 or rw4 model instead. (can be done via generic, but I haven't tried it)
>
> Finn
>
> On Thu, 27 Jan 2022 at 13:08, Sylvan Benaksas <sylva...@gmail.com> wrote:
> > Hi Finn,
> >
> > can you advise how it is possible to limit rw2 model from overfitting, in frequentist gams using mgcv I would do this by reducing the number of knots for a spline,
> >
> > many thanks,
> > Sylvan
> >
> >
> > On Tuesday, January 25, 2022 at 11:26:25 PM UTC finn.l...@gmail.com wrote:
> > > Use a rw2 model, which is the continuous Bayesian process model linked to the spline penalty given by the integral of the squared second order derivative. Most inla examples with nonlinear covariate effects use that model, so there should be plenty of examples.
> > >
> > > Finn
> > >
> > > > On 25 Jan 2022, at 23:14, David Dayi Li <davi...@gmail.com> wrote:
> > > >
> > > > ﻿Hi,
> > > >
> > > > I have been reading about the following paper:  Accounting for preferential sampling in species distribution models by Pennino et al.
> > > >
> > > > In their model construction, they mentioned using a Bayesian smoothing spline to model the nonlinear effect of the covariate. I am trying to do a similar thing for my problem but I am not exactly sure how to incorporate a smoothing spline for the covariates in my model. Mostly, I am not familiar with the syntax and there does not seem to be any example code online for doing this.
> > > >
> > > > Can someone provide any help for this? Thank you very much!
> > > >
> > > > Best,
> > > > David
